This is a comprehensive Exception Handling Strategy designed specifically for your JournalService architecture.
Based on my analysis, your application has a robust "Happy Path" and performance optimizations (Async, Redis, Oracle Parallelism), but it lacks a unified strategy to handle failures gracefully. The current approach relies heavily on generic try-catch (Exception e) blocks or raw framework exceptions, which can lead to inconsistent API responses and silent failures in Async threads.
Here is the complete implementation plan.
Part 1: The Core Infrastructure
First, we need a standardized response structure. This ensures that whether a validation fails, a DB locks, or a file is corrupt, the frontend always receives the same JSON format.
1. Standard Error Response DTO
Create this in com.fincore.JournalService.Dto.Error:
package com.fincore.JournalService.Dto.Error;

import lombok.Builder;
import lombok.Data;
import java.time.LocalDateTime;
import java.util.List;

@Data
@Builder
public class ApiErrorResponse {
    private LocalDateTime timestamp;
    private int status;
    private String error;
    private String message;
    private String path;
    private List<ValidationError> validationErrors; // Only for 400 Bad Request

    @Data
    @Builder
    public static class ValidationError {
        private String field;
        private String message;
    }
}

2. Semantic Custom Exceptions
Do not use generic RuntimeException. Create these in com.fincore.JournalService.Exception to represent specific domain failures.
package com.fincore.JournalService.Exception;

import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ResponseStatus;

// 1. For File I/O, Parsing, or Corrupt Excel/CSV data
@ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST)
public class FileProcessingException extends RuntimeException {
    public FileProcessingException(String message, Throwable cause) { super(message, cause); }
    public FileProcessingException(String message) { super(message); }
}

// 2. For Redis Cache Misses or expired tokens
@ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.GONE)
public class CacheExpiredException extends RuntimeException {
    public CacheExpiredException(String message) { super(message); }
}

// 3. For Race Conditions (e.g., Double click on Batch Creation)
@ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.CONFLICT)
public class ConcurrencyLockException extends RuntimeException {
    public ConcurrencyLockException(String message) { super(message); }
}

// 4. For Database integrity issues (Foreign keys, Data truncation)
@ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR)
public class DatabaseOperationException extends RuntimeException {
    public DatabaseOperationException(String message, Throwable cause) { super(message, cause); }
}

// 5. For External Service failures (HDFS/Hive)
@ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.BAD_GATEWAY)
public class ExternalServiceException extends RuntimeException {
    public ExternalServiceException(String message) { super(message); }
}

Part 2: The Global Exception Handler
This is the "Brain" of your error handling. It intercepts exceptions from all Controllers and formats them into your ApiErrorResponse.
Create com.fincore.JournalService.Exception.GlobalExceptionHandler:
package com.fincore.JournalService.Exception;

import com.fincore.JournalService.Dto.Error.ApiErrorResponse;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import lombok.extern.slf4j.Slf4j;
import org.springframework.dao.DataAccessException;
import org.springframework.dao.DataIntegrityViolationException;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.validation.FieldError;
import org.springframework.web.bind.MethodArgumentNotValidException;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ExceptionHandler;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestControllerAdvice;
import org.springframework.web.multipart.MaxUploadSizeExceededException;

import java.time.LocalDateTime;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

@RestControllerAdvice
@Slf4j
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    // --- 1. VALIDATION ERRORS (@Valid) ---
    @ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiErrorResponse> handleValidation(MethodArgumentNotValidException ex, HttpServletRequest request) {
        List<ApiErrorResponse.ValidationError> errors = ex.getBindingResult().getFieldErrors().stream()
                .map(err -> ApiErrorResponse.ValidationError.builder()
                        .field(err.getField())
                        .message(err.getDefaultMessage())
                        .build())
                .collect(Collectors.toList());

        return buildResponse(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST, "Validation Failed", "Input validation error", request.getRequestURI(), errors);
    }

    // --- 2. FILE UPLOAD ERRORS ---
    @ExceptionHandler(MaxUploadSizeExceededException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiErrorResponse> handleMaxSize(MaxUploadSizeExceededException ex, HttpServletRequest request) {
        return buildResponse(HttpStatus.PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE, "File Too Large", "Maximum upload size exceeded", request.getRequestURI(), null);
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(FileProcessingException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiErrorResponse> handleFileProcessing(FileProcessingException ex, HttpServletRequest request) {
        log.error("File Processing Error: {}", ex.getMessage());
        return buildResponse(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST, "File Error", ex.getMessage(), request.getRequestURI(), null);
    }

    // --- 3. CONCURRENCY & LOCKS ---
    @ExceptionHandler({ConcurrencyLockException.class, IllegalStateException.class}) // Catching IllegalStateException as you used it for locks
    public ResponseEntity<ApiErrorResponse> handleConcurrency(RuntimeException ex, HttpServletRequest request) {
        log.warn("Concurrency Conflict: {}", ex.getMessage());
        return buildResponse(HttpStatus.CONFLICT, "Operation Conflict", ex.getMessage(), request.getRequestURI(), null);
    }

    // --- 4. RESOURCE NOT FOUND ---
    @ExceptionHandler(ResourceNotFoundException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiErrorResponse> handleNotFound(ResourceNotFoundException ex, HttpServletRequest request) {
        return buildResponse(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND, "Not Found", ex.getMessage(), request.getRequestURI(), null);
    }

    // --- 5. CACHE / EXPIRED DATA ---
    @ExceptionHandler(CacheExpiredException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiErrorResponse> handleCacheExpired(CacheExpiredException ex, HttpServletRequest request) {
        return buildResponse(HttpStatus.GONE, "Session Expired", ex.getMessage(), request.getRequestURI(), null);
    }

    // --- 6. DATABASE ERRORS ---
    @ExceptionHandler(DataIntegrityViolationException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiErrorResponse> handleSqlIntegrity(DataIntegrityViolationException ex, HttpServletRequest request) {
        log.error("DB Integrity Error: ", ex);
        return buildResponse(HttpStatus.CONFLICT, "Data Integrity Error", "Constraint violation or duplicate entry.", request.getRequestURI(), null);
    }
    
    @ExceptionHandler(DataAccessException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiErrorResponse> handleDbError(DataAccessException ex, HttpServletRequest request) {
        log.error("Database Error: ", ex);
        return buildResponse(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR, "Database Error", "A database error occurred. Please contact support.", request.getRequestURI(), null);
    }

    // --- 7. CATCH-ALL ---
    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiErrorResponse> handleGeneral(Exception ex, HttpServletRequest request) {
        log.error("Unhandled Exception: ", ex);
        return buildResponse(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR, "Internal Server Error", "An unexpected error occurred.", request.getRequestURI(), null);
    }

    private ResponseEntity<ApiErrorResponse> buildResponse(HttpStatus status, String error, String message, String path, List<ApiErrorResponse.ValidationError> validationErrors) {
        ApiErrorResponse response = ApiErrorResponse.builder()
                .timestamp(LocalDateTime.now())
                .status(status.value())
                .error(error)
                .message(message)
                .path(path)
                .validationErrors(validationErrors)
                .build();
        return new ResponseEntity<>(response, status);
    }
}

Part 3: Handling Async Exceptions
Your code heavily relies on @Async. CRITICAL WARNING: Exceptions thrown in @Async void methods do not go to the GlobalExceptionHandler. They are swallowed by the thread unless handled.
You must create an AsyncConfig extension to catch these.
Modify com.fincore.JournalService.config.AsyncConfig:
// Add imports
import org.springframework.aop.interceptor.AsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.AsyncConfigurer;
import lombok.extern.slf4j.Slf4j;
import java.lang.reflect.Method;

@Configuration
@EnableAsync
@Slf4j
public class AsyncConfig implements AsyncConfigurer { // Implements AsyncConfigurer

    // ... existing bulkExecutor bean ...

    @Override
    public AsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler getAsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler() {
        return (Throwable ex, Method method, Object... params) -> {
            log.error("CRITICAL ASYNC FAILURE in method: {}", method.getName(), ex);
            // Optional: Send alert to monitoring system here
        };
    }
}

Part 4: Targeted Code Enhancements
Now we apply the custom exceptions to your services. I will point out exactly where to replace generic exceptions with specific ones.
1. JournalRequestServiceImpl.java
Location: createBatchFromCacheAsync
Change: Catch the IllegalStateException for locks and verify it propagates to the Controller (where the Global Handler will catch it and return 409).
Location: executeAsyncBatchCreation (Async Method)
Current: Catches Exception, logs it, cleans up.
Gap: If journalBulkValidationService.getValidRowsFromCache returns null, you log "Cache Expired".
Fix: Explicitly handle the "Not Found" case in the synchronous part if possible, or ensure the error status is descriptive.
Refinement for processChunkRaw (JDBC Batching):
Currently, you throw RuntimeException("DB Error", e).
Improvement: Throw DatabaseOperationException to differentiate DB errors from logic errors.
// In processChunkRaw method:
catch (SQLException e) {
    log.error("Raw JDBC Batch Insert Failed for chunk starting at " + globalOffset, e);
    // Use the custom exception
    throw new DatabaseOperationException("Failed to persist batch chunk due to SQL error", e);
}

2. JournalBulkValidationService.java
This service does heavy I/O.
Location: initiateValidation
Current: Throws IOException.
Change: Wrap Files.copy in a try-catch and throw FileProcessingException.
// In initiateValidation:
try {
    Files.copy(fileStream, tempFile, StandardCopyOption.REPLACE_EXISTING);
} catch (IOException e) {
    throw new FileProcessingException("Failed to save uploaded file to temporary storage", e);
}

Location: getValidRowsFromCache
Current: Returns Collections.emptyList() on error.
Risk: The caller (JournalRequestService) sees an empty list and might just do nothing or fail silently.
Fix: Throw CacheExpiredException if data is missing, so the UI gets a clear "Session Expired" error instead of a generic failure.
// In getValidRowsFromCache:
byte[] compressed = redisTemplate.opsForValue().get("DATA_" + requestId);
if (compressed == null) {
    throw new CacheExpiredException("Validation data not found. The cache may have expired (TTL 1 Hour). Please re-upload.");
}

3. JournalRequestController.java
Location: createBatchFromCache
Current: Manually catches IllegalStateException and returns 409.
Change: Remove the try-catch block entirely. Let the exception propagate to the GlobalExceptionHandler. This reduces code duplication in controllers.
Before:
} catch (IllegalStateException e) {
    return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CONFLICT).body(...);
}

After:
// The GlobalExceptionHandler will now handle ConcurrencyLockException / IllegalStateException
// and return the standardized 409 response automatically.
String batchId = journalRequestService.createBatchFromCacheAsync(...);
return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.ACCEPTED).body(...);

4. HdfsSyncService.java
Location: syncToDataLake
Current: Catches Exception and logs "HDFS SYNC FAILED".
Fix: This is correct for background tasks, but ensure you differentiate between DataAccessException (retryable) and logic errors.
Summary of Benefits
 * Consistency: Every error (validation, DB, logic) returns the exact same JSON structure (ApiErrorResponse), making Frontend integration trivial.
 * Visibility: The AsyncConfig change ensures that if a background thread dies, it is logged loudly as "CRITICAL ASYNC FAILURE" rather than vanishing.
 * Clarity: Replacing RuntimeException with ConcurrencyLockException or CacheExpiredException makes the logs and the HTTP status codes immediately understandable (409 vs 410 vs 500).
 * Resilience: By catching specific IO exceptions in the Validation service, you prevent file system issues from masquerading as application bugs.
